WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.420
Good to have you on SpaceX Daily, episode 23.

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:05.580
I'm Patrick in Vancouver.

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:08.620
Today is July 5th, 2026.

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:11.460
From the launch pad to the trading floor,

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:13.340
here's everything that moved today.

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:16.100
Ship 40's completed static fire

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:19.440
and a semiconductor test bed writing today's Falcon 9,

00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:23.020
both test hardware that must survive repeated thermal

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:25.180
and vibration loads before scaling.

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:29.140
Ship 40 completed a full-duration static fire,

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:31.620
confirming the vehicle's six Raptor engines

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:33.840
can sustain the burn profile required

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:35.540
for the upcoming flight test.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.600
The test moves the ship one step closer

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:41.320
to stacking and launch preparations at starbase.

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:44.440
Engineers will now review telemetry

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:47.680
for any thrust vector or propellant flow anomalies

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:49.660
before clearing the vehicle for flight.

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:52.620
The result keeps the Starship campaign

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:54.460
on its current cadence target.

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:58.520
Remember, the show covered Starship on episode 22.

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:01.360
Today's static fire moves that forward

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:03.380
by clearing another hardware milestone

00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:05.140
on the path to booster catch

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:07.440
and orbital refueling demonstrations.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:10.780
While Starship hardware advances on the ground,

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:13.000
a routine Falcon 9 mission today

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:16.240
tested an entirely different class of orbital hardware.

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:19.260
Two semiconductor manufacturing pods

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:22.200
from Bessxar Space Industries are manifested

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:26.200
as a rideshare on the Starlink 10 to 50 Falcon 9 mission.

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:28.780
The pods will operate in orbit

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:31.400
to evaluate microgravity, crystal growth,

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:33.700
and thin film deposition processes.

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:37.260
Data returned will inform whether orbital conditions

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:40.800
can reduce defect rates compared with terrestrial fabs.

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:42.980
The test marks an early attempt

00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:45.940
to fly production-relevant semiconductor hardware

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:48.620
rather than pure research payloads.

00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:52.120
The Falcon 9 lifted 29 Starlink satellites

00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:56.280
plus the Bessx semiconductor test bed from SLC 40.

00:01:56.860 --> 00:02:00.340
The first stage booster performed its usual recovery sequence

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:01.780
after deploying the stack.

00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:05.520
This mission continues the rapid replenishment cadence

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:08.140
needed to maintain global coverage density.

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:11.580
The liftoff of the Starlink 10 to 50 mission

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:13.660
from Space Launch Complex 40

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:18.160
is currently scheduled for 6.50 AM Eastern daylight time.

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:20.820
The launch window and trajectory support

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:23.220
both the primary constellation deployment

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:25.520
and the secondary semiconductor payload.

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:29.100
The mission profile calls for a southeast trajectory

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:31.140
that places the Starlink satellites

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:34.340
into a 550 kilometers circular orbit.

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:38.000
Recovery of the booster on the drone ship is planned,

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:40.660
preserving the vehicle for a future flight.

00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:44.760
Several accounts highlighted the exact T-0 timing

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:47.760
of 6.50 AM Eastern daylight time

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:51.420
and the expected booster landing coordinates on the drone ship.

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:54.260
The same mission also carried a second,

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:57.060
more ambitious orbital processing experiment

00:02:57.060 --> 00:02:59.140
that SpaceX disclosed this week.

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:03.340
SpaceX disclosed the AI-1 satellite architecture

00:03:03.980 --> 00:03:06.460
which integrates onboard processing hardware

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:09.220
with Starlink's laser inter-satellite links.

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:12.640
The design targets orbital inference workloads

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:16.120
that could reduce latency for Earth-based AI users.

00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:19.300
Details were released in regulatory filings

00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:21.240
tied to the company's public listing.

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:24.760
Observers on X noted the unusual pairing

00:03:24.760 --> 00:03:26.760
of production semiconductor hardware

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:28.860
with a routine Starlink deployment

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:32.020
and are tracking early temperature readings from the pods.

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:36.040
A separate thread discussed how the AI-1 satellites

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:38.620
disclosed processing nodes could interface

00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:41.060
with existing Starlink laser terminals

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:42.660
for distributed inference.

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:46.140
Launch watchers posted high-resolution photos

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:49.340
of the transporter erector at SLC-40,

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:51.760
showing the additional mounting hardware

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:53.100
for the best-gar pods.

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:55.220
Worth keeping an eye on,

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:58.860
the semiconductor test bed remains an unproven manufacturing

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:02.700
approach whose yield improvements must still be demonstrated

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:04.520
after recovery and analysis.

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:08.160
Any contamination or thermal control shortfall

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:09.800
during the short orbital stay

00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:13.040
could limit the data's usefulness for scaling decisions.

00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:17.200
Follow-on flights will be required to establish repeatability

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:19.700
before investors treat orbital fabs

00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:21.280
as a material revenue line.

00:04:22.020 --> 00:04:26.320
On the AI front, SpaceX disclosed the AI-1 satellite design

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:29.740
which places processing hardware directly on orbit

00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:32.720
and routes data through laser inter-satellite links

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:34.580
already flying on Starlink.

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:38.500
The architecture is intended to support low-latency

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:41.180
inference tasks without routing every query

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:42.400
through ground stations.

00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:44.620
From an engineering standpoint,

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:47.160
placing semiconductor fabrication hardware

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:49.820
and orbit removes gravity-driven convection

00:04:49.820 --> 00:04:53.020
and sedimentation that distort crystal growth on Earth.

00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:56.940
The best-gar pods therefore test whether microgravity

00:04:56.940 --> 00:05:00.980
can produce lower defect densities in thin films and bulls,

00:05:01.180 --> 00:05:04.340
directly attacking the idiot index of terrestrial

00:05:04.340 --> 00:05:06.860
clean room, energy, and filtration costs.

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:10.300
If the orbital environment cuts defect rates

00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:13.740
by even a factor of two, the raw material floor

00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:17.300
for each finished wafer drops because fewer scrapped crystals

00:05:17.300 --> 00:05:18.500
are needed upstream.

00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:22.000
The Falcon 9 secondary payload slot

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:24.420
keeps marginal launch cost near zero,

00:05:24.940 --> 00:05:27.980
letting the experiment run without dedicated heavy lift

00:05:27.980 --> 00:05:28.580
economics.

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:31.740
Success would open a path to larger orbital

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:34.020
foundries whose power and thermal constraints

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:36.800
are solved by the same Starship class vehicles

00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:40.080
already under test, and a quick market note.

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:46.320
SPCX closed at $162, up 2.6%.

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:49.920
Before we go, watch for the first post-flight data drop

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:53.520
from the best-X pods once the Starlink X to 50 stack

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:56.220
reaches its 550-kilometer orbit.

00:05:56.940 --> 00:06:00.140
That covers everything worth knowing about SpaceX today.

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:06.640
SPCX is trading at $162, up 2.6%.

